body:

# Tor Updates

## People want privacy. The web keeps getting in their way.

As the web is actively trying to penalize users for seeking out privacy, more
people than ever are pushing back... 50k+ Tor Browser users reveal what they
really need from the ‘world wide web'. 53% use Tor to avoid ISP surveillance,
while more than one in five say their online activity could expose them to
violence. That corresponds to the number of Tor's Android users, making Tor
Browser for mobile the gateway to Tor's future growth–AND a critical
chokepoint. With 47% reporting website breakage and annoying CAPTCHAs as a
drawback, it's clear that private browsing should not come with a penalty.

Can Tor meet those challenges? Find out more in our recent blog post about The
future of private browsing:
https://blog.torproject.org/the-future-of-private-browsing

## Tor VPN Beta: What we've learned building our own VPN for Android from scratch

Last fall, we soft-launched Tor VPN Beta as a way to extend Tor's privacy
protections beyond the browser to an entire Android device. Since then, we've
been able to learn from how people are actually using it in real-world
scenarios. Early adoption was driven by users in high-risk environments like
Iran, Turkmenistan and across the Global South where people are facing severe
network restrictions. One thing we noticed, because of the way commercial VPN
providers advertise their censorship circumvention capabilities, Tor VPN beta
users were trying to pick exit locations before connecting. But Tor's design is
different and what users really needed were bridges instead. So we designed
around that.

Learn more about these and other learnings, and how we've improved the app
since launch here: https://blog.torproject.org/tor-vpn-beta/

## New download pages to better navigate the Tor ecosystem, and be sure you're getting what you bargained for

To make it easier to browse and identify which Tor app is right for a user's
needs, we have refreshed our download pages, now featuring a comprehensive
comparison table that reveals each app's privacy and anonymity properties at a
single glance. And to further combat look-alike and spoofing apps, and ensure
that high-risk users access authentic Tor apps, we made the page's URL
consistent with the syntax we use across other official Tor websites.

Can't quite picture it? No problem, navigate to
https://download.torproject.org, give it a spin, and let us know what you
think.

## Call for applications: Tor Community Fellowship

The Tor Project is happy to announce the launch of the Tor Community
Fellowship. For its first year, we are accepting 4 human rights defenders,
journalists, technologists, and researchers.

Fellows will get the chance to learn from the Tor Project team, build a deep
understanding of how internet censorship works, how tools like Tor work to
support circumvention at the network level, and develop skills in technical
investigations in relation to Tor. Fellows will conclude with a small project
on a related subject of their choice.

This is a part-time fellowship which will span 6 months, from February to July 2027.
Fellows will be offered a stipend of 500 USD per month for the duration of the
fellowship.

Applications are open until December 17, 2026:
https://nc.torproject.net/apps/forms/s/Dtk65kWacFaq8ejwsXYSsZmr

## People like you help millions of people stay private and anonymous online

At any given moment, 4M+ people are using the Tor network... but only less than
7,000 people donate every year. Can you help us bring that number up and chip
in?

Find out what your donation enables here:
https://blog.torproject.org/tor-is-powered-by-you/

# New Releases

## Tor Browser 16.0

With this new release, we've introduced cleaner navigation, clearer
descriptions and warnings for settings that could compromise user privacy. The
redesigned settings page makes adjusting security levels, bridge selection and
other circumvention settings easier. On Android, large parts of the interface
have received a facelift as well with an update to Material Design 3, bringing
a more modern look and feel that's consistent with Android's latest design
language. To help keep your browsing experience even more private, we've
introduced an optional setting that replaces specific page titles with a
generic "Tor Browser" label.  Find out how to activate this feature and browse
through all of the changes we've made to this first stable release based on
Firefox ESR 153: https://blog.torproject.org/new-release-tor-browser-160/

## Arti 2.8.0 released, 3 November 2026

This release continues our ongoing development towards using Arti as a relay
and as a directory authority, with much of the work focusing on computing the
consensus from votes, bootstrapping router descriptors and microdescriptors, as
well as relay bandwidth rate-limiting, descriptor encoding, and the relay DNS
cache implementation. Additionally, we have made a number of cleanups and
clarifications to the Tor specification.

Release notes: https://blog.torproject.org/arti_2_8_0_released/

## Tails 7.17.1, November 17, 2026

This release is an emergency release to fix a critical security vulnerability
in uBlock Origin , an ad blocking extension in Tor Browser.

Release notes: https://blog.torproject.org/new-release-tails-7_17_1/

# Upcoming events

## State of the Onion 2026

The State of the Onion is the Tor Project's annual virtual fundraiser where we
share updates from Tor Project's teams and community, highlighting Tor's work
and the impact it has made across the globe during the year.

### December 2nd, 2026 – News & updates from the Tor Project

Join us as we celebrate 20 years of the Tor Project. Get a first look at our
work to bring Signal to Tails, hear what we've learned from internet shutdowns
in Iran, and see how we're gearing up to get Arti, our implementation of Tor in
Rust that powers Tor VPN among others, production ready. Save the date to get
notified: https://www.youtube.com/live/fx8oN5D5biU.

### December 9th, 2026 - Updates from the Tor Community

Meet the people building on Tor and advancing privacy through free and
open-source software. At State of the Onion 2026 Community Day, learn more
about OONI's new censorship measurement tools, the Guardian Project's efforts
to improve the Tor experience on iOS, and the many ways you can volunteer to
help others get online with Bloco's Snowflake Volunteer app. The program also
features speakers from Women on Web, Hush Line, Cake Wallet, and many more
sharing highlights from their work in 2026. Join Tor's Community Team as they
guide you through the program. Save the date to get notified:
https://www.youtube.com/live/1CNyyfIlPnQ

Streams are available on YouTube, X, as an Onion Service at 1700 UTC on both
days, and as a replay on the Tor Project's YouTube Channel

## Chaos Computer Congress – C40C3

Chaos Computer Congress 2026 (40C3) is the largest European hacker party,
conference and platform for technology, society and civil rights.

Meet members of the Tor Project's network, anticensorship, and outreach teams
who'll be participating, presenting, and selling exclusive merchandise.

